RULES = {
    3: "Each piece of operational or process content has exactly one authoritative source -- a cfg_* row where one exists, otherwise one named document. Any other document or response needing that content points to the authoritative source by name/section; it does not restate, paraphrase, or duplicate it. A rule may not exist in both a document and a cfg_* row at the same time -- once a rule is captured in cfg_*, its document version is replaced with a pointer, not left standing alongside it. The home of each kind of content is cfg_control_home (the control map): content lives in its kind's home only.",
    14: "An ad-hoc SQL scratch file worth keeping lives under scripts/SQLite/{IBA_DB,Research_DB}/ (one subfolder per database it targets). Named for what it actually checks, hyphens not spaces/underscores (naming-shape), never left untitled. A file worth re-running is committed like any other script; a genuinely one-off query that has been answered is archived (scripts/archive/), not deleted and not left cluttering the live folder.",
    33: "An ongoing investigation or tracked-item register is one living document, updated in place as it evolves, and a second competing document for the same tracked set is never started. A reversed or replaced item is removed from the live register and kept in its history home (the register's change records, an archived version, or git), not struck through or annotated in place (rule replaced-history-out-of-read-path).",
    41: "Any module that a researcher or Claude operates by hand -- not purely internal library code called only by other code -- has a dedicated PS entry point under `governance.scripts_ps_dir` (`iba/app/ps/`). A module reachable only via raw Python or raw SQL is a usability and audit gap.",
    42: "A change to a tool, module, or user-facing behaviour is not complete until `USER-GUIDE.md` reflects it, in the same unit of work -- `governance.User_Guide_scope` states what the guide must cover (\"the latest state of all the tools\"); this rule states when it's updated.",
    43: "Every active PS script under `governance.scripts_ps_dir` (`iba/app/ps/`) that performs a real project operation dispatches through `run.py` (`python -m iba.app.run <package> --step <step> --run-id <id>`) -- not a direct call into the underlying Python module. Dispatching through run.py is what gives an operation a real `run` row, a `run_id` and `cfg_step` registration (and module_blocking protection once that gate is redesigned and re-enabled: it is disabled since #859); a script that calls its module directly leaves no record anywhere that it ran, when, or with what result. Two named exceptions, both permanent and architectural, not violations: (1) `Start-Iba.ps1` -- necessarily, since it bootstraps what run.py itself depends on; (2) `Escalation.ps1`'s write actions (`-Action Raise/Update/AnswerRun/Correction/ResolveSelfCorrectable/EscalateToDecision/Match/ThreadCheck`) -- a deliberate manual front door onto the escalation system's own backlog workflow, not a pipeline run (its report actions `-Action List/History/Thread/Export` DO dispatch through run.py, work package `escalation-reporting`). Any other bypass is real non-compliance, not a third exception -- tracked as its own escalation, not silently accepted.",
    45: "AnswerRun's flat approve/reject/revise/hold/noted vocabulary is refused for both resolution_kind values. A decision_required item is answered through Update's vocabulary (ready_for_approval -> approved; -State on-hold; next_action=reject/revise/noted). A self_correctable item is closed through ResolveSelfCorrectable, or converted through EscalateToDecision. A decision_required run terminates and never resumes; a fresh run is the test.",
    46: "Each module or utility, from now on (case-by-case as it comes up in development, NOT retrofitted to existing modules), must have a test plan. The test plan covers every meaningfully different interaction, parameter, and option combination of that module/utility's intended functionality -- not a single happy-path example. The test plan is a living artifact: when a functional component changes, the test plan for that component is updated in the same unit of work, before the change is considered complete -- the same 'same unit of work' discipline governance.build_md_on_code_change already applies to BUILD.md. The test plan is RUN after the design is approved and the build (per the approved plan) is complete -- a required stage inside the existing plan/propose/design (in detail) -> approve -> build per the plan -> approve cycle, not a separate optional step. The test results are included IN the build's escalation resolution when requesting final approval -- not just claimed as 'tested live' in prose without the actual per-case results shown, and not omitted. A written-but-unexecuted test is indistinguishable from no test at all.",
    49: "A covered target row's version column is not an incrementing per-item counter -- it is a literal foreign key to record_change_log.id, the log row describing that row's own most recent change.",
    51: "A hard (physical) delete of DB rows is normally disallowed (the standing no-physical-delete-in-automated-flows convention) but is permitted as a one-time, explicitly-instructed migration action. Each occurrence needs its own explicit researcher instruction; this rule records the pattern, it doesn't pre-authorise future hard deletes generically.",
    60: "A tool's own auto-generated report output (an escalation list/history export, CONFIG-REPORT.md, a validation run's report, a content-index rebuild report -- anything a *.report_path/*.output_dir cfg_setting names) stays exactly where that setting points. An AUTHORED deliverable document (a plan, a design doc, a gap analysis, an investigation write-up) that is produced FOR one specific escalation's work belongs under outputs/escalation/, not iba/docs/. iba/docs/ is for engineering/planning documentation that is NOT tied to one specific escalation (architecture notes, cross-cutting design references).",
    61: "If a table, column, or row is marked inactive in the configs (cfg_table.inactive / cfg_column.inactive / an active=0/inactive=1/delete_flagged=1 row), it is never included in any report, analysis, or result -- unless the request explicitly asks to explore historical/inactive data. Going back over inactive/historical data to rebuild or reconcile it, unprompted, is itself a violation of this rule's intent, not a form of thoroughness -- it wastes time and produces confusing results. Default to ignoring inactive state entirely; only engage with it on an explicit historical-exploration ask.",
    62: "Any change that affects config -- a table created/altered, a script/step added, a setting/enum introduced -- updates its own cfg_* registration (cfg_table/cfg_column for schema; cfg_utility/cfg_step/cfg_write_grant for operations; cfg_setting/cfg_enum for content) in the SAME unit of work as the change itself, not left for the researcher to discover later. configmaint.validate runs find_unregistered_tables_and_columns every time (live-schema vs cfg_table/cfg_column diff, both databases), which verifies this.",
    63: "An escalation item sitting at next_action_assigned_to=Claude in an open working state (review/in-progress) has exactly ONE legitimate way to hand it to the Researcher: set next_action=ready_for_approval together with next_action_assigned_to=Researcher and a resolution, all in the SAME call -- whether the resolution states a completed fix awaiting approval, or an open question needing the researcher's decision on how to proceed. There is no bare-reassignment or bounce-back-via-review path. The same applies to an item Claude finds itself unsure whether to apply even after approval (state=approved): reset it via Correction to next_action=ready_for_approval, next_action_assigned_to=Researcher, with the conflict stated plainly in the resolution. Before any hand-over for approval, Claude checks three things and the hand-over states them: (1) what exactly the researcher is asked to approve; (2) that it is ready for approval, i.e. a finished piece of work or a decision with its options, not something Claude still has to do or work the researcher has told Claude to carry; (3) the resolution states plainly what approval is sought. If none of the three holds, the item is not handed over: work already under way stays with Claude with a one-line acknowledgement, and clearing the Stop-hook backlog check is never a reason to hand over. An interim approval (approval of one step whose result needs a further action by Claude) is handed over with -NeedsFollowup 1 in the same call, so the researcher's `approved` routes the item back to Claude (re-assigned) and does not close a step still in progress.",
    64: "An escalation-tied working file (a design doc, scope/reconciliation doc, checklist, worked example, or similar authored deliverable produced to progress one specific escalation's work -- in Workflow/Catalogue/, iba/docs/, outputs/escalation/ or an equivalent authored-deliverable folder) carries that escalation's numeric id as its filename prefix ('{id}-{descriptive-name}...'), as the auto-generated '{id}-escalation-history.md' report does. Does NOT apply to living/standing documents (GOVERNANCE.md, CHARTER.md, USER-GUIDE.md, BUILD.md, slash-command definitions under .claude/commands/) or to reusable scripts -- only escalation-scoped authored deliverables.",
    67: "bible_research.db (research_db) is never included in any IBA-line ANALYSIS result -- report, escalation finding, cross-check, or answer to the researcher drawing conclusions about the study's content -- EXCEPT the prose tables (prose_section, prose_section_type, and cfg_prose_concept's referenced content), the canonical, foundational authority on the programme's own governing concepts (governance.prose_canonical_authority), explicitly exempt from this exclusion, AND EXCEPT registered DB-hygiene/maintenance utilities (e.g. purge.audit/purge.execute) whose job is administrative housekeeping across the whole app -- soft-delete counts, dependency checks, row removal -- rather than analytical investigation: these may query, report on, and write to bible_research.db tables as their registered function requires. All other current base-data/lexical/cluster investigation and reporting is scoped to iba.db only. If a question or discrepancy seems to require bible_research.db data OUTSIDE the prose tables or a registered maintenance utility's own scope, stop and flag it rather than querying or citing that database.",
    68: "Every correction to ib_observation (update, withdraw, consolidate) happens ONLY as a byproduct of record_one_observation() (iba/app/lib/recordingpass.py) processing a real, fresh LLM answer for the exact occurrence in question -- never a standalone script that edits or derives content for existing rows without a genuine new answer behind it. Reconciliation is therefore inherently incremental: a verse's stale/duplicate data only gets cleaned up when that verse is genuinely reprocessed (lexical.meaning -Force) -- there is no batch-cleanup lever and none should be built.",
    73: "A strand pulls verses by its M-code, but an M-code tag does not make a word the verse's characteristic: cluster allocation is a loose association (rule 70) and a word's role -- characteristic, qualifier / process-element, or standalone -- is decided per occurrence (verse-analysis method s13-14; glossary 'role'). Each verse is read in its passage and genre; every M-coded word (rule 71, set-based) is given its role against the verse's main IB operation; only then is the strand's own word read, in the role it has there. A strand word in the qualifier role is carried as a qualifier of that characteristic (no own account from that verse, method s13b), recorded as an interrelationship and a face (rule 89), and the verse's home follows the characteristic. The M-code words are read in relation to each other (relational observation), pointers outside the verse kept separate, parties and route recorded (rule 72), following the confirmed role-driven sequence and progressive reading across strands. Collections are grouped by main operation, not entry word. Existing narrative is not audited in a dedicated pass; placements are corrected in each strand's read-back as the verse's characteristic M-code comes into focus. Reading also: (1) goes outward from the verse, not inward from the item's question -- first write down everything the verse carries, each with where it bears (chapter, thread, signpost), and answer the item's question last; (2) for every word that names an inner or bodily act, looks at the original -- the root's range (strong_meaning_tree and the ESV renderings across its verses) and whether the English adds or hides something; (3) never closes an item or verse on 'not X' -- the close records what the verse is instead. A role label is never a verdict. Full guide: Workflow/Instructions/wa-verse-reading-by-role-guide-v2-20261019.md.",
    74: "The open threads register is carried in the escalation table as type='thread' (alias OT-nn), not edited as a markdown file. A thread is not worked as a task: it moves only when a strand reaches it (focused capture, rule 84). Its items (escalation_thread_item; cluster, Strong's, verse, strand, chapter, event, researcher, escalation) are what a strand or task is matched against (Escalation.ps1 -Action Match). Claude manages each thread's lifecycle; the researcher gives feedback in chat, which Claude records verbatim the same turn, and there is no approval step. Every response to an item -- touched, narrowed, forward, ruling, note, closed, reopened, withdrawn, superseded -- is an escalation_thread_item_response row, recorded through Escalation.ps1 -Action Update -Id OT-nn -Item ... -ResponseKind ... -Respond ..., and written in full to the thread's history. A thread completes when its last open item is settled; a thread is withdrawn or superseded (-SupersededBy OT-nn) with its open items settled in the same call. Notes and comments on a thread are Updates, never edits to the register file. The file _analytics/cross-cluster-web/open-threads-register.md is generated (Escalation.ps1 -Action Export -Type thread) and regenerated at session-close when a thread row changed in the session (SC12). Its quality, validity and integrity are audited at every start (Escalation.ps1 -Action ThreadAudit). Before every unit pull of a strand, the threads that bear on it are matched (Escalation.ps1 -Action Match -Cluster .. -Strong .. -Verse ..; -For nnnn records the match); held items and researcher questions are raised as threads at every ledger approval (Escalation.ps1 -Action Raise -Type thread).",
    75: "Developer Mode removes configmaint.propose's per-row approval gate, never the config audit. Every cfg_* write made in a Developer Mode session (a migration, a one-off script) goes through iba.app.handlers.configmaint.apply_logged(conn, run_id, table, op, where, set), the same audited write propose uses after approval, so cfg_change_detail records the table, op, where, set, the row's prior state and when. The run_id names the session's work (e.g. DEVMODE-<escalation>-<what>-<date>).",
    77: "Every narrative statement that goes beyond its verses has a narrative claim register entry with verse sources, or it is removed from the narrative (it may return later in another form). The interpretation of verses in context sets the narrative; verses that sit against a narrative line change the narrative and get their own claim, never trimmed, set aside or left out. Readings of the words in focus check the full verse set: a verse in neither the narrative nor the register is a missing reading area. The register anchors what verses say from different readings and focal points; the narrative tells the story from the claims; the register keeps the narrative honest. When an update touches a chapter, the existing passages on the concept being updated and its related concepts (not the whole chapter) are read against the register under this rule in the same pass, and that concept's current claims that no passage tells are linked, restated or withdrawn.",
}

